-- target: branch
--
-- W1-PROV — the four PROVISIONER REGISTRIES the rehearsal branch did not have.
--
-- WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
-- --------------------
-- §3 rule 36 levels the branch's SCHEMA with production, and `W0-SYNC` did that. It does
-- not level ROWS, and four of the tables `platform.provision` reads are registries whose
-- ROWS are the behaviour, not data:
--
--   · `platform.provision_rule_message`   — production 89 rows, branch 0. Every refusal is
--     built from a row here, so on the branch EVERY finding came back as
--     "the refusal table has no message for this rule" — a real refusal wearing a bug
--     report, which is the opposite of §4.8's four-part message.
--   · `platform.provision_generate_target` — production 58 rows, branch 0. THE PARITY RULE
--     ("provisionable only if the repository can generate for it") reads this table, so on
--     the branch every schema was `identity.schema.not_generable` and NOTHING could be
--     provisioned at all.
--   · `platform.provision_schema`          — production 2 rows, branch 0. The spec's JSON
--     schema. With no row, `jsonschema_validation_errors(NULL, spec)` returns nothing and
--     the shape check was a silent no-op: a spec production refuses for an unknown key
--     passed here.
--   · `iam.entity_read_kernel_expected()`  — NOT a row and NOT levelled here; it is
--     repaired in `w1_prov_provisioner_speaks_partitions.sql`, because the defect is in the
--     FINGERPRINT, not in the branch (see that file's header).
--
-- Every row below is production's own, read SELECT-only 2026-09-17 and written verbatim.
-- Nothing here is a fixture: the branch fixture this lane needs (`custom` as a generate
-- target) is a separate file, `w1_prov_branch_custom_generate_target.sql`, so that this one
-- can be re-derived from production at any time and diffed against it.
--
-- BRANCH ONLY, and it needs no production half: production already holds every row.
-- THE INVERSE: `migrations/inverse/w1_prov_branch_registry_levelling_down.sql`.

set lock_timeout = '2s';
set statement_timeout = '120s';

